vo_avfoundation: clear and blend the OSD canvas for less

With heavily styled ASS (karaoke, signs, \move; a new frame of subtitles
for every video frame) the inline OSD layer cost an iPhone Air's E cores
20.9 % of a core against 3.2 % for the same file without subtitles. The VO
thread spent 70 % of its time in the libass-over-BGRA blend and 21 % in
memset clearing the 2736x1260 canvas, on every frame.

Clearing: each pool buffer remembered the union of what was last drawn into
it, so a sign at the top and dialogue at the bottom cleared the whole canvas
between them, and only 4 buffers were tracked while the pool cycles 5-9 of
them (the layer holds a 128 ms queue lead plus the shown and the cached
sample): every buffer came back unknown and was cleared whole, 13.8 MB per
frame. A buffer now records up to 16 rects (avf_dirty_set: rects merge only
when one box is no more pixels to clear than both, the cheapest merge makes
room when full) and 12 buffers are tracked. A tracked surface is released as
soon as the pool has freed it, rather than pinned for 2 s.

Blending: the NEON kernel divided every channel by 65025 through a 64-bit
magic multiply. It now uses one saturating doubling multiply-high
(floor(y/65025) == y * ceil(2^46/65025) >> 46 for y < 2^24); an opaque
color (the common case) drops to 16-bit math, since 255 factors out of the
division; an all-255 mask group with an opaque color stores the color
without reading the canvas; zero groups are skipped with a scalar test.
Bit-exact with the scalar reference: checked once over every mask, color,
alpha and destination value, and guarded by scripts/test_avf_osd_blend.sh.

On the iPhone Air (anime-ass.mkv, vo=avfoundation, hwdec=videotoolbox,
interleaved n=3) CPU drops from 20.9 to 15.6 % of a core and CPU energy by
31 %, with the footprint unchanged. osd-perf now reports the pixels cleared, the
full clears and the tracked buffers.

